      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Provider\nA provider is a plugin: a separate program\nthat Terraform downloads and starts. The\nprovider knows the API of one product.\nTerraform decides what to do and in which\norder, and the provider makes the API calls.\nFor Snowflake, the provider builds SQL\nstatements such as `CREATE WAREHOUSE`\n([provider source](https://github.com/snowflak\nedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2.23.0/\npkg/sdk/warehouses_gen.go#L51-L55)).\n\nEach provider has a source address of the form\n`[<hostname>/]<namespace>/<type>`. The\nhostname defaults to `registry.terraform.io`,\nso `snowflakedb/snowflake` means\n`registry.terraform.io/snowflakedb/snowflake`.\nThe namespace is the publisher, and the type\nis the product ([source addresses](https://dev\neloper.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/provid\ners/requirements#source-addresses)).\n\nTwo blocks set up a provider. The `terraform`\nblock says which provider and which versions\nto use. The `provider` block says how to\nconnect. These blocks come from About the\nExamples:\n\n```hcl\nterraform {\nrequired_version = \">= 1.5\"\n\nrequired_providers {\nsnowflake = {\nsource  = \"snowflakedb/snowflake\"\nversion = \"2.23.0\"\n}\n}\n}\n\nprovider \"snowflake\" {\norganization_name = var.snowflake_organization\naccount_name      = var.snowflake_account\nuser              = var.snowflake_user\nrole              = var.snowflake_role\nauthenticator     = \"SNOWFLAKE_JWT\"\nprivate_key       = file(var.private_key_path)\n}\n```\n\nTerraform acts as this user with this role.\nThe role's privileges therefore limit what an\napply can do in Snowflake. `SNOWFLAKE_JWT`\nselects key-pair authentication, which the\n[authentication\nmap](../authentication/authentication.md)\nexplains. The credentials come from input\nvariables, so the `.tf` files hold no secret.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-90c5d1358d12",
      "originalText": "Provider\nA provider is a plugin: a separate program that Terraform downloads and starts. The provider knows the API of one product. Terraform decides what to do and in which order, and the provider makes the API calls. For Snowflake, the provider builds SQL statements such as `CREATE WAREHOUSE` ([provider source](https://github.com/snowflakedb/terraform-provider-snowflake/blob/v2.23.0/pkg/sdk/warehouses_gen.go#L51-L55)).\n\nEach provider has a source address of the form `[<hostname>/]<namespace>/<type>`. The hostname defaults to `registry.terraform.io`, so `snowflakedb/snowflake` means `registry.terraform.io/snowflakedb/snowflake`. The namespace is the publisher, and the type is the product ([source addresses](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/providers/requirements#source-addresses)).\n\nTwo blocks set up a provider. The `terraform` block says which provider and which versions to use. The `provider` block says how to connect. These blocks come from About the Examples:\n\n```hcl\nterraform {\n  required_version = \">= 1.5\"\n\n  required_providers {\n    snowflake = {\n      source  = \"snowflakedb/snowflake\"\n      version = \"2.23.0\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nprovider \"snowflake\" {\n  organization_name = var.snowflake_organization\n  account_name      = var.snowflake_account\n  user              = var.snowflake_user\n  role              = var.snowflake_role\n  authenticator     = \"SNOWFLAKE_JWT\"\n  private_key       = file(var.private_key_path)\n}\n```\n\nTerraform acts as this user with this role. The role's privileges therefore limit what an apply can do in Snowflake. `SNOWFLAKE_JWT` selects key-pair authentication, which the [authentication map](../authentication/authentication.md) explains. The credentials come from input variables, so the `.tf` files hold no secret.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "provider"
      }
    },
