      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Variables, Locals, and Outputs\nThese three blocks are the inputs, the\ninternal names and the results of a module:\n\n- A `variable` block declares an input. Code\nreads it as `var.<name>`.\n- A `locals` block names an expression, so\nthat you write it once. Code reads it as\n`local.<name>`.\n- An `output` block returns a value. The root\nmodule prints its outputs after an apply and\nkeeps them in the state. A parent module reads\nthe output of a child as\n`module.<name>.<output>`.\n\nThese blocks come from About the Examples:\n\n```hcl\nvariable \"environment\" {\ntype        = string\ndescription = \"Short name of the environment,\nsuch as dev or prod.\"\n}\n\nlocals {\nsuffix = upper(var.environment)\n}\n\noutput \"raw_database_name\" {\nvalue = snowflake_database.raw.name\n}\n```\n\nThe root module gets variable values from\nseveral sources. When two sources set the same\nvariable, the first source in this list wins\n([assign values](https://developer.hashicorp.c\nom/terraform/language/values/variables#assign-\nvalues-to-variables)):\n\n1. `-var` and `-var-file` options on the\ncommand line, in the order given.\n2. `*.auto.tfvars` and `*.auto.tfvars.json`\nfiles, in lexical order.\n3. The file `terraform.tfvars.json`.\n4. The file `terraform.tfvars`.\n5. Environment variables named\n`TF_VAR_<name>`.\n6. The `default` argument of the `variable`\nblock.\n\nA child module gets its values from the\narguments of its `module` block. The argument\n`sensitive = true` hides a value in the plan\noutput. Terraform still stores the value in\nthe state ([sensitive values](https://develope\nr.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/values/vari\nables#sensitive-values-in-variables)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-41e049809498",
      "originalText": "Variables, Locals, and Outputs\nThese three blocks are the inputs, the internal names and the results of a module:\n\n- A `variable` block declares an input. Code reads it as `var.<name>`.\n- A `locals` block names an expression, so that you write it once. Code reads it as `local.<name>`.\n- An `output` block returns a value. The root module prints its outputs after an apply and keeps them in the state. A parent module reads the output of a child as `module.<name>.<output>`.\n\nThese blocks come from About the Examples:\n\n```hcl\nvariable \"environment\" {\n  type        = string\n  description = \"Short name of the environment, such as dev or prod.\"\n}\n\nlocals {\n  suffix = upper(var.environment)\n}\n\noutput \"raw_database_name\" {\n  value = snowflake_database.raw.name\n}\n```\n\nThe root module gets variable values from several sources. When two sources set the same variable, the first source in this list wins ([assign values](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/values/variables#assign-values-to-variables)):\n\n1. `-var` and `-var-file` options on the command line, in the order given.\n2. `*.auto.tfvars` and `*.auto.tfvars.json` files, in lexical order.\n3. The file `terraform.tfvars.json`.\n4. The file `terraform.tfvars`.\n5. Environment variables named `TF_VAR_<name>`.\n6. The `default` argument of the `variable` block.\n\nA child module gets its values from the arguments of its `module` block. The argument `sensitive = true` hides a value in the plan output. Terraform still stores the value in the state ([sensitive values](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/values/variables#sensitive-values-in-variables)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "variables-locals-outputs"
      }
    },
