      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7497940553,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-754a08ddf8bc",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1310.4,
      "y": 2720.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 892.5,
      "seed": 369965,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Backend\nA backend decides where the state lives.\nWithout a `backend` block, the state is the\nlocal file `terraform.tfstate`. A team keeps\nthe state in a shared, remote place, so that\nevery run sees the same state. Terraform has\nbackends for S3, Azure Storage (`azurerm`),\nGoogle Cloud Storage (`gcs`), PostgreSQL\n(`pg`), Kubernetes and others ([backends](http\ns://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language\n/backend)). A `cloud` block in place of a\n`backend` block stores the state in HCP\nTerraform, the hosted service of HashiCorp\n([terraform block](https://developer.hashicorp\n.com/terraform/language/block/terraform)).\nThis is an S3 backend:\n\n```hcl\nterraform {\nbackend \"s3\" {\nbucket       = \"my-terraform-state\"\nkey          = \"snowflake/terraform.tfstate\"\nregion       = \"us-west-2\"\nuse_lockfile = true\n}\n}\n```\n\nA backend block cannot use variables, locals\nor data sources. To change the backend for\neach environment, a repo leaves some arguments\nout of the block and gives them to `terraform\ninit`, for example `terraform init\n-backend-config=prod.s3.tfbackend`. HashiCorp\ncalls this a partial configuration and\nrecommends the file name `*.<backend\nname>.tfbackend` ([partial configuration](http\ns://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language\n/backend#partial-configuration)).\n\nA lock stops two runs from writing the same\nstate at the same time ([state locking](https:\n//developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/s\ntate/locking)). With `use_lockfile = true`,\nthe S3 backend writes a lock file next to the\nstate, named `<key>.tflock`. Some repos set\n`dynamodb_table` in place of it, which locks\nthrough a DynamoDB table. The docs mark\nDynamoDB locking as deprecated ([S3 backend](h\nttps://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/langu\nage/backend/s3)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-754a08ddf8bc",
      "originalText": "Backend\nA backend decides where the state lives. Without a `backend` block, the state is the local file `terraform.tfstate`. A team keeps the state in a shared, remote place, so that every run sees the same state. Terraform has backends for S3, Azure Storage (`azurerm`), Google Cloud Storage (`gcs`), PostgreSQL (`pg`), Kubernetes and others ([backends](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/backend)). A `cloud` block in place of a `backend` block stores the state in HCP Terraform, the hosted service of HashiCorp ([terraform block](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/block/terraform)). This is an S3 backend:\n\n```hcl\nterraform {\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket       = \"my-terraform-state\"\n    key          = \"snowflake/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region       = \"us-west-2\"\n    use_lockfile = true\n  }\n}\n```\n\nA backend block cannot use variables, locals or data sources. To change the backend for each environment, a repo leaves some arguments out of the block and gives them to `terraform init`, for example `terraform init -backend-config=prod.s3.tfbackend`. HashiCorp calls this a partial configuration and recommends the file name `*.<backend name>.tfbackend` ([partial configuration](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/backend#partial-configuration)).\n\nA lock stops two runs from writing the same state at the same time ([state locking](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/state/locking)). With `use_lockfile = true`, the S3 backend writes a lock file next to the state, named `<key>.tflock`. Some repos set `dynamodb_table` in place of it, which locks through a DynamoDB table. The docs mark DynamoDB locking as deprecated ([S3 backend](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/backend/s3)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "backend"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 9022607015,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-a279fe916f63",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1298.4,
      "y": 2980.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "#F8FAFC",
      "width": 418.8,
      "height": 220,
      "seed": 397249,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3,
        "value": 8
