    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 1646835496,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-bed97175b06e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1310.4,
      "y": 3370.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 1347.5,
      "seed": 379667,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Plan\nA plan is the list of actions that makes the\nreal objects match the configuration. A plan\nchanges no real object. Each action has a\nsymbol ([plan output](https://developer.hashic\norp.com/terraform/cli/commands/plan#understand\n-plan-output)):\n\n| Symbol | Action | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `+` | Create | The object does not exist.\nTerraform creates it. |\n| `~` | Update in place | Terraform changes\nthe object without deleting it. |\n| `-` | Destroy | Terraform deletes the\nobject. |\n| `-/+` | Replace | Terraform deletes the\nobject and then creates a new one. |\n\nThe plan in About the Examples creates a\ndatabase and a warehouse in Snowflake. This is\nthe warehouse part of its output and the\nsummary:\n\n```text\n# snowflake_warehouse.transform will be\ncreated\n+ resource \"snowflake_warehouse\" \"transform\" {\n+ auto_resume                         =\n\"default\"\n+ auto_suspend                        = 60\n+ enable_query_acceleration           =\n\"default\"\n+ fully_qualified_name                = (known\nafter apply)\n+ id                                  = (known\nafter apply)\n+ max_concurrency_level               = (known\nafter apply)\n+ name                                =\n\"TRANSFORM_DEV_WH\"\n+ parameters                          = (known\nafter apply)\n+ query_acceleration_max_scale_factor = -1\n+ show_output                         = (known\nafter apply)\n+ statement_queued_timeout_in_seconds = (known\nafter apply)\n+ statement_timeout_in_seconds        = (known\nafter apply)\n+ warehouse_size                      =\n\"XSMALL\"\n}\n\nPlan: 2 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy.\n```\n\n`(known after apply)` marks a value that only\nthe API can give, such as the ID. The values\nfrom the configuration, such as the name,\nappear in full. `\"default\"` and `-1` are\nspecial values of the Snowflake provider. They\nmean \"use the Snowflake default\" ([warehouse r\nesource](https://registry.terraform.io/provide\nrs/snowflakedb/snowflake/latest/docs/resources\n/warehouse)).\n\n`terraform plan` alone makes a speculative\nplan, which is a preview. `terraform plan\n-out=dev.tfplan` saves the plan in a file, and\n`terraform apply dev.tfplan` then makes\nexactly those changes ([plan command](https://\ndeveloper.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands\n/plan)). The saved file in About the Examples\nis a zip archive of 4,375 bytes. It holds the\nplan, two state snapshots, a copy of each\n`.tf` file and the lock file.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-bed97175b06e",
      "originalText": "Plan\nA plan is the list of actions that makes the real objects match the configuration. A plan changes no real object. Each action has a symbol ([plan output](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/plan#understand-plan-output)):\n\n| Symbol | Action | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `+` | Create | The object does not exist. Terraform creates it. |\n| `~` | Update in place | Terraform changes the object without deleting it. |\n| `-` | Destroy | Terraform deletes the object. |\n| `-/+` | Replace | Terraform deletes the object and then creates a new one. |\n\nThe plan in About the Examples creates a database and a warehouse in Snowflake. This is the warehouse part of its output and the summary:\n\n```text\n  # snowflake_warehouse.transform will be created\n  + resource \"snowflake_warehouse\" \"transform\" {\n      + auto_resume                         = \"default\"\n      + auto_suspend                        = 60\n      + enable_query_acceleration           = \"default\"\n      + fully_qualified_name                = (known after apply)\n      + id                                  = (known after apply)\n      + max_concurrency_level               = (known after apply)\n      + name                                = \"TRANSFORM_DEV_WH\"\n      + parameters                          = (known after apply)\n      + query_acceleration_max_scale_factor = -1\n      + show_output                         = (known after apply)\n      + statement_queued_timeout_in_seconds = (known after apply)\n      + statement_timeout_in_seconds        = (known after apply)\n      + warehouse_size                      = \"XSMALL\"\n    }\n\nPlan: 2 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy.\n```\n\n`(known after apply)` marks a value that only the API can give, such as the ID. The values from the configuration, such as the name, appear in full. `\"default\"` and `-1` are special values of the Snowflake provider. They mean \"use the Snowflake default\" ([warehouse resource](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/snowflakedb/snowflake/latest/docs/resources/warehouse)).\n\n`terraform plan` alone makes a speculative plan, which is a preview. `terraform plan -out=dev.tfplan` saves the plan in a file, and `terraform apply dev.tfplan` then makes exactly those changes ([plan command](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/plan)). The saved file in About the Examples is a zip archive of 4,375 bytes. It holds the plan, two state snapshots, a copy of each `.tf` file and the lock file.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "plan"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
