      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Outside Changes Get Reverted\nTerraform makes each argument that it manages\nmatch the configuration. A plan therefore\nreverts a change that someone made outside\nTerraform, for example in Snowsight. This is\nthe first plan after the import in About the\nExamples. The real warehouse had `AUTO_SUSPEND\n= 60`, and the configuration said 300. The\n`show_output` lines are left out:\n\n```text\nsnowflake_warehouse.agent: Refreshing state...\n[id=\"AGENT_WH\"]\n\n# snowflake_warehouse.agent will be updated\nin-place\n~ resource \"snowflake_warehouse\" \"agent\" {\n~ auto_resume                         = \"true\"\n-> \"default\"\n~ auto_suspend                        = 60 ->\n300\n~ enable_query_acceleration           = \"true\"\n-> \"default\"\n- generation                          = \"2\" ->\nnull\nid                                  =\n\"\\\"AGENT_WH\\\"\"\n- max_cluster_count                   = 1 ->\nnull\n- min_cluster_count                   = 1 ->\nnull\nname                                =\n\"AGENT_WH\"\n~ query_acceleration_max_scale_factor = 8 ->\n-1\n- scaling_policy                      =\n\"STANDARD\" -> null\n- warehouse_type                      =\n\"STANDARD\" -> null\n# (8 unchanged attributes hidden)\n}\n\nPlan: 0 to add, 1 to change, 0 to destroy.\n```\n\nOnly the `auto_suspend` line comes from the\nconfiguration. The other eight lines are\narguments that the configuration does not set:\n\n- The three `~` lines get the provider's value\n`\"default\"` or `-1`, which means the Snowflake\ndefault. For this warehouse, the Snowflake\ndefaults are the current values: `AUTO_RESUME`\nis `TRUE`, `ENABLE_QUERY_ACCELERATION` is\n`TRUE` for a standard warehouse, and\n`QUERY_ACCELERATION_MAX_SCALE_FACTOR` is 8\n([CREATE WAREHOUSE](https://docs.snowflake.com\n/en/sql-reference/sql/create-warehouse)). A\nwarehouse with a value other than the default\nwould lose that value.\n- The five `-` lines set an argument to null.\nFor `warehouse_type`, the provider docs say\nthat the provider then sets the type to\n`STANDARD`. For the other four, the docs do\nnot say what an apply sends to Snowflake\n([warehouse resource](https://registry.terrafo\nrm.io/providers/snowflakedb/snowflake/latest/d\nocs/resources/warehouse)).\n\nWrite each setting that matters into the\nconfiguration, so that the plan shows no line\nfor it. To keep a change made outside\nTerraform, copy the value into the\nconfiguration. To make Terraform leave one\nargument alone, add `lifecycle {\nignore_changes = [auto_suspend] }` ([lifecycle\n](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/la\nnguage/meta-arguments/lifecycle)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-918b95bd5e1c",
      "originalText": "Outside Changes Get Reverted\nTerraform makes each argument that it manages match the configuration. A plan therefore reverts a change that someone made outside Terraform, for example in Snowsight. This is the first plan after the import in About the Examples. The real warehouse had `AUTO_SUSPEND = 60`, and the configuration said 300. The `show_output` lines are left out:\n\n```text\nsnowflake_warehouse.agent: Refreshing state... [id=\"AGENT_WH\"]\n\n  # snowflake_warehouse.agent will be updated in-place\n  ~ resource \"snowflake_warehouse\" \"agent\" {\n      ~ auto_resume                         = \"true\" -> \"default\"\n      ~ auto_suspend                        = 60 -> 300\n      ~ enable_query_acceleration           = \"true\" -> \"default\"\n      - generation                          = \"2\" -> null\n        id                                  = \"\\\"AGENT_WH\\\"\"\n      - max_cluster_count                   = 1 -> null\n      - min_cluster_count                   = 1 -> null\n        name                                = \"AGENT_WH\"\n      ~ query_acceleration_max_scale_factor = 8 -> -1\n      - scaling_policy                      = \"STANDARD\" -> null\n      - warehouse_type                      = \"STANDARD\" -> null\n        # (8 unchanged attributes hidden)\n    }\n\nPlan: 0 to add, 1 to change, 0 to destroy.\n```\n\nOnly the `auto_suspend` line comes from the configuration. The other eight lines are arguments that the configuration does not set:\n\n- The three `~` lines get the provider's value `\"default\"` or `-1`, which means the Snowflake default. For this warehouse, the Snowflake defaults are the current values: `AUTO_RESUME` is `TRUE`, `ENABLE_QUERY_ACCELERATION` is `TRUE` for a standard warehouse, and `QUERY_ACCELERATION_MAX_SCALE_FACTOR` is 8 ([CREATE WAREHOUSE](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/sql/create-warehouse)). A warehouse with a value other than the default would lose that value.\n- The five `-` lines set an argument to null. For `warehouse_type`, the provider docs say that the provider then sets the type to `STANDARD`. For the other four, the docs do not say what an apply sends to Snowflake ([warehouse resource](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/snowflakedb/snowflake/latest/docs/resources/warehouse)).\n\nWrite each setting that matters into the configuration, so that the plan shows no line for it. To keep a change made outside Terraform, copy the value into the configuration. To make Terraform leave one argument alone, add `lifecycle { ignore_changes = [auto_suspend] }` ([lifecycle](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/meta-arguments/lifecycle)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "outside-changes-get-reverted"
      }
    },
