      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Root Module Folders\nEach folder in which someone runs Terraform is\na root module with its own state. Two signs\nmark such a folder:\n\n- A `backend` or `cloud` block, which says\nwhere its state lives.\n- A committed `.terraform.lock.hcl`. Terraform\nwrites the lock file in the folder where\n`init` runs, and the style guide says to\ncommit it. Some repos do not commit it, so the\nbackend block is the more reliable sign.\n\nThese commands list the candidates from the\nrepo root:\n\n```bash\nfind . -name .terraform.lock.hcl -not -path\n'*/.terraform/*'\ngrep -rl --include='*.tf' -E '^\\s*(backend\n\"|cloud \\{)' .\n```\n\nA folder that other folders only call through\n`module` blocks is a child module. It needs\nneither sign, because it uses the backend and\nthe lock file of the root module that calls\nit. The standard module structure puts such\nmodules under `modules/` ([standard module str\nucture](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraf\norm/language/modules/develop/structure)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-bc7479f3a7eb",
      "originalText": "Root Module Folders\nEach folder in which someone runs Terraform is a root module with its own state. Two signs mark such a folder:\n\n- A `backend` or `cloud` block, which says where its state lives.\n- A committed `.terraform.lock.hcl`. Terraform writes the lock file in the folder where `init` runs, and the style guide says to commit it. Some repos do not commit it, so the backend block is the more reliable sign.\n\nThese commands list the candidates from the repo root:\n\n```bash\nfind . -name .terraform.lock.hcl -not -path '*/.terraform/*'\ngrep -rl --include='*.tf' -E '^\\s*(backend \"|cloud \\{)' .\n```\n\nA folder that other folders only call through `module` blocks is a child module. It needs neither sign, because it uses the backend and the lock file of the root module that calls it. The standard module structure puts such modules under `modules/` ([standard module structure](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/modules/develop/structure)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "root-module-folders"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
