      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Reading Order\nThis order answers the main questions about a\nconfiguration with the least reading:\n\n1. Find the root modules. Root Module Folders\nshows how.\n2. In each root module, read the `terraform`\nblock. It names the providers, their versions\nand the backend, so you learn which products\nthe folder changes and where the state lives.\n3. Read the `provider` blocks. They name the\naccount, the user and the role that Terraform\nacts as, and where the credentials come from.\n4. Read `variables.tf` and the `*.tfvars`\nfiles. They show what differs between\nenvironments.\n5. List the resource types. This command\ncounts the blocks in a folder:\n\n`grep -rhoE '^(resource|data|module) \"[^\"]+\"'\n--include='*.tf' . | sort | uniq -c`\n\nIn the example of About the Examples, it\nprints `1 resource \"snowflake_database\"` and\n`1 resource \"snowflake_warehouse\"`.\n6. Follow each `module` block to its `source`\nfolder, and read that module in the same\norder.\n7. Read the CI pipeline file, if the repo has\none. It shows which folder and which workspace\neach job uses, and which step needs an\napproval before apply.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-10e41087749c",
      "originalText": "Reading Order\nThis order answers the main questions about a configuration with the least reading:\n\n1. Find the root modules. Root Module Folders shows how.\n2. In each root module, read the `terraform` block. It names the providers, their versions and the backend, so you learn which products the folder changes and where the state lives.\n3. Read the `provider` blocks. They name the account, the user and the role that Terraform acts as, and where the credentials come from.\n4. Read `variables.tf` and the `*.tfvars` files. They show what differs between environments.\n5. List the resource types. This command counts the blocks in a folder:\n\n   `grep -rhoE '^(resource|data|module) \"[^\"]+\"' --include='*.tf' . | sort | uniq -c`\n\n   In the example of About the Examples, it prints `1 resource \"snowflake_database\"` and `1 resource \"snowflake_warehouse\"`.\n6. Follow each `module` block to its `source` folder, and read that module in the same order.\n7. Read the CI pipeline file, if the repo has one. It shows which folder and which workspace each job uses, and which step needs an approval before apply.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "reading-order"
      }
    },
