      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "terraform apply\n#### Inputs\n\n- A saved plan file. Terraform then makes\nexactly the planned changes, without a prompt.\n- Or no file. Terraform then makes a new plan,\nshows it, and waits for you to type `yes`.\n`-auto-approve` skips that question ([apply co\nmmand](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terrafo\nrm/cli/commands/apply)).\n- Credentials with the privileges to make the\nchanges.\n\n#### Outputs\n\n- Changed real objects, through the API calls\nof the providers. For Snowflake, the calls are\nSQL statements.\n- A new state in the backend.\n- The values of the root module outputs, on\nthe screen.\n\n#### Under the hood\n\nApply holds the state lock while it runs. When\nan API call fails, Terraform reports the\nerror, saves the state with the changes that\nit already made, unlocks the state and stops.\nIt does not roll back the finished changes.\nAfter you fix the cause, you run apply again\n([errors during apply](https://developer.hashi\ncorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/apply#errors-d\nuring-an-apply-operation)). With HCP\nTerraform, a speculative plan runs for each\npull request, and an apply starts after the\nmerge ([speculative plans on pull requests](ht\ntps://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cloud-\ndocs/workspaces/run/ui#speculative-plans-on-pu\nll-requests)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-f740d3cd1c67",
      "originalText": "terraform apply\n#### Inputs\n\n- A saved plan file. Terraform then makes exactly the planned changes, without a prompt.\n- Or no file. Terraform then makes a new plan, shows it, and waits for you to type `yes`. `-auto-approve` skips that question ([apply command](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/apply)).\n- Credentials with the privileges to make the changes.\n\n#### Outputs\n\n- Changed real objects, through the API calls of the providers. For Snowflake, the calls are SQL statements.\n- A new state in the backend.\n- The values of the root module outputs, on the screen.\n\n#### Under the hood\n\nApply holds the state lock while it runs. When an API call fails, Terraform reports the error, saves the state with the changes that it already made, unlocks the state and stops. It does not roll back the finished changes. After you fix the cause, you run apply again ([errors during apply](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/apply#errors-during-an-apply-operation)). With HCP Terraform, a speculative plan runs for each pull request, and an apply starts after the merge ([speculative plans on pull requests](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cloud-docs/workspaces/run/ui#speculative-plans-on-pull-requests)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "terraform-apply"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
